    "https://news.bryant.edu/bryant-rises-9th-nationally-career-services-16th-best-run-college-2027-princeton-review-rankings": {
      "title": "Main navigation",
      "description": "Bryant rises to 9th nationally for career services, 16th best-run college in 2027 Princeton Review rankings | Bryant News Skip to Content Main navigation Bryant News Academics Community Global Students Alumni Bryant Magazine Media Academics Community Global Students Alumni Bryant Magazine Media Bryant rises to 9th nationally for career services, 16th best-run college in 2027 Princeton Review rankings Sep 03, 2026,\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\tby\n\t\t\t\tCasey Nilsson SMITHFIELD, R.I. — Bryant University rose to ninth in the nation for Best Career Services in The Princeton Review’s latest college rankings, joining a series of national distinctions for the university and its student experience. Bryant also debuted on several ranked lists this year, earning the 16 th spot for Best-Run Colleges, 16 th for Happiest Students, and 23 rd for Friendliest Students. The Douglas and Judith Krupp Library ranks 19 th nationally for Best College Library, and Bryant debuted at number seven for strong town relations. The university was once again named to The Princeton Review’s Best Value Colleges, Best Northeast, Green Colleges, and The Best 392 Colleges lists. “These rankings capture what distinguishes Bryant,” says President Ross Gittell, Ph.D. “Our students receive a transformative education, strong preparation for their careers, a deeply connected community where they can thrive, and a return on their educational investment in the top 1 percent nationally. Being named one of the nation’s best-run colleges is also a credit to the entire university and the collective commitment to student success and institutional excellence.” Bryant’s rise to ninth in the nation for Best Career Services marks a 16-spot increase from last year’s ranking. The Princeton Review bases its rankings on student assessments of their colleges’ career and job placement services. In their survey responses, Bryant students cited Bryant’s Amica Center for Career Education as one of the university’s greatest strengths. “The team in the career center does an excellent job helping students find job/internships and prepare for interviews,” one student told The Princeton Review. Others point to “so many great career networking opportunities” on and off campus. The Amica Center for Career Education works with students from their first year through Commencement, providing personalized career coaching, internship and employment support, mock interviews, resume assistance, career fairs, and connections with employers and alumni. First-year students begin that work through Bryant’s “Career “Students come to Bryant with big ambitions, hopes, and passions, and they find a community that supports them,” said Inge-Lise Ameer, Ed.D., vice president for student affairs and dean of students. “That happens through the career coaches, employer connections, internships, and careers upon graduation they find at the Amica Center, and through the friendships, mentors, organizations, and experiences that shape their lives on campus. These rankings are especially meaningful because they come directly from students and speak to the experience they have here.\" According to student respondents, “The Bryant community is very tight knit and helps make you feel at home on campus.” Students described their peers as “motivated, hardworking, kind, fun” and said that while Bryant students are driven, they support one another and “push each other to be better.” Students also described an active campus with a myriad of ways to get involved and meet people. One student said extracurricular activities “have introduced me to a new group of people and exposed me to new ways of thinking.” Another described Bryant as “extremely welcoming,” with the “perfect balance of academics, recreation, and extracurricular activities.” In their responses, students also illustrated a community of “different types of leaders who are each striving for their individual goals but are also devoted to maintaining a strong, cohesive student body.” Bryant’s seventh-in-the-nation ranking for town relations recognizes the university’s relationship with Smithfield and surrounding Rhode Island communities. Bryant students, faculty, and staff participate in community partnerships, events, and other programs throughout the region, including an annual Service Day when faculty and staff volunteer with local nonprofits and community organizations. The Princeton Review rankings follow other national recognitions for Bryant student outcomes. In August, LinkedIn ranked Bryant 41 st in the nation on its 2026 Top Colleges list, which recognizes 50 U.S. colleges for the long-term career success of their graduates. LinkedIn considers job placement, internship and recruiter demand, network strength, career advancement, and knowledge breadth. TIME also ranked Bryant in the top 100 nationally for career outcomes. No. 9 — Best Career Services No. 19 — Best College Library No. 16 — Best-Run Colleges No. 23 — Friendliest Students No. 16 — Happiest Students No. 7 — Town Relations Best Value Colleges Best Northeast Green Colleges The Best 392 Colleges About Bryant University For 163 years, Bryant University has been at the forefront of delivering an exceptional education that anticipates the future and prepares students to be innovative leaders of character in a changing world. The university delivers an integrated academic and student life experience, with nationally recognized academic programs at the intersection of business, liberal arts, and health and behavioral sciences, while embedding applied artificial intelligence and experiential learning across the curriculum. Located on a 465-acre contemporary campus in Smithfield, R.I., Bryant enrolls over 4,000 undergraduate and graduate students from 41 states and 50 countries. Bryant is recognized as a top one percent national leader in student education outcomes and ROI and is highly ranked by U.S. News and World Report , The Wall Street Journal , QS Stars, Money , Poets&Quants, and College Factual. Bryant.edu Read More Academics Campus Life Career ROI Student Success Related Stories Finding their freedom: New, first-of-its kind book helps teens struggling with an eating disorder Bryant’s Christina Tortolani, co-authors address eating disorder recovery gap through impactful resource At Convocation 2026, first-year students begin to ‘write the next chapter of Bryant University’ Campus community celebrates Class of 2030 in time-honored tradition Co-op programs give students extended experience in the workplace Six-month, full-time stints with employers offer undergrads more responsibility and leadership opportunities Bryant recognized for nationwide AI leadership by Center for Public Sector AI Center’s AI 50 names Bryant as only four-year university among this year’s organizational honorees What pension payout option should you choose? New research shows why health status matters ‘If the retiree is very healthy, then they may want to choose annuity,’ says Bryant’s Jessica Zhai, Ph.D. Bryant researcher examines how women, men respond to unequal workloads versus unequal earnings ‘Equipping managers with tools to identify and address workload disparities is essential,’ says Xiaofei Pan View More 1150 Douglas Pike, Smithfield, RI 02917 (401) 232-6000 Bryant Websites Bryant.edu Info Directory Undergraduate Admission Graduate Admission Bryant Bulldogs Publications Bryant Magazine Engage WJMF Privacy Policy Accessibility Emergency Information ©2026 The Trustees of Bryant University. All rights reserved."
    },
    "https://news.bryant.edu/bryants-college-business-receives-high-marks-academic-experience-career-outcomes-2026-poetsquants": {
      "title": "Main navigation",
      "description": "Bryant’s College of Business receives high marks for academic experience, career outcomes in 2026 Poets&Quants survey | Bryant News Skip to Content Main navigation Bryant News Academics Community Global Students Alumni Bryant Magazine Media Academics Community Global Students Alumni Bryant Magazine Media Bryant’s College of Business receives high marks for academic experience, career outcomes in 2026 Poets&Quants survey Mar 30, 2026,\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\tby\n\t\t\t\tPeter Kerwin SMITHFIELD, RI—A year after initially breaking into the Poets&Quants survey of top undergraduate business schools in the country, Bryant University’s undergraduate College of Business was ranked #15 for academic experience and #45 for career outcomes in the 2026 survey. The national survey considers admissions standards, student feedback on academic experience, and career outcomes in determining the rankings. “Bryant continues to be recognized as a leader among the nation’s top undergraduate business schools, providing a transformational education that drives exceptional career outcomes,” says President Ross Gittell, Ph.D. “Our strong academic experience ranking is a testament to our faculty’s deep commitment to student outcomes and a curriculum that empowers students to think strategically, collaborate boldly, and lead with purpose.” “This survey is a recognition of intentional curriculum and learning experiences designed by our faculty, highlighting the success that comes from giving students the opportunity to tackle real-world challenges in an environment that fosters innovation and collaboration,” said Bryant’s Provost and Chief Academic Officer Rupendra Paliwal, Ph.D. “It’s about preparing students for what’s next, providing hands-on learning, global experiences, and high-tech tools that allow them to graduate as purpose-driven leaders ready to make a powerful impact from day one.” College of Business Dean Todd Alessandri, Ph.D., suggests the latest Poets&Quants findings are just the latest reminder of Bryant’s growing national and international standing. “Since Bryant’s notable emergence in the Poets&Quants rankings last year, the institution has continued to be recognized for our outstanding faculty and interdisciplinary learning approach that provides students with real-world experiences, analytical skills, and the confidence to know they are ready to achieve their career goals.” To learn more about Bryant’s College of Business, visit bryant.edu/college-business . About Bryant University For more than 160 years, Bryant University has been at the forefront of delivering an exceptional education that anticipates the future and prepares students to be innovative leaders of character in a changing world. The university delivers an integrated academic and student life experience with nationally recognized academic programs at the intersection of business, liberal arts, and health and behavioral sciences. Located on a 465-acre contemporary campus in Smithfield, R.I., Bryant enrolls approximately 3,800 undergraduate and graduate students from 41 states and 50 countries. Bryant is recognized as a top 1 percent national leader in student education outcomes and ROI and regularly receives high rankings from U.S. News and World Report, QS Stars, Money, Bloomberg Businessweek, Wall Street Journal, and College Factual. Bryant.edu Read More Academics Career College of Business Experiential Learning President Provost ROI Related Stories Finding their freedom: New, first-of-its kind book helps teens struggling with an eating disorder Bryant’s Christina Tortolani, co-authors address eating disorder recovery gap through impactful resource At Convocation 2026, first-year students begin to ‘write the next chapter of Bryant University’ Campus community celebrates Class of 2030 in time-honored tradition Bryant rises to 9th nationally for career services, 16th best-run college in 2027 Princeton Review rankings University also earns national recognition for institutional excellence, student experience, academic resources Co-op programs give students extended experience in the workplace Six-month, full-time stints with employers offer undergrads more responsibility and leadership opportunities Bryant recognized for nationwide AI leadership by Center for Public Sector AI Center’s AI 50 names Bryant as only four-year university among this year’s organizational honorees What pension payout option should you choose? New research shows why health status matters ‘If the retiree is very healthy, then they may want to choose annuity,’ says Bryant’s Jessica Zhai, Ph.D. View More 1150 Douglas Pike, Smithfield, RI 02917 (401) 232-6000 Bryant Websites Bryant.edu Info Directory Undergraduate Admission Graduate Admission Bryant Bulldogs Publications Bryant Magazine Engage WJMF Privacy Policy Accessibility Emergency Information ©2026 The Trustees of Bryant University. All rights reserved."
    }
